WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.113
The exercise imagines a social feed backed by users,
follows and tweets tables.

00:00:05.113 --> 00:00:08.219
Publishing one tweet is initially a small write.

00:00:08.219 --> 00:00:14.691
Reading a feed requires finding followed authors and
their recent posts, then assembling the result.

00:00:14.691 --> 00:00:21.098
Joins are not inherently slow, but broad access
across many authors or shards can make this request

00:00:21.098 --> 00:00:24.399
expensive compared with retrieving a prepared feed.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.732
A materialized feed moves work from reading time to
publication time.

00:00:28.732 --> 00:00:34.007
When an author posts, workers add a reference or
entry to followers’ prepared feeds.

00:00:34.007 --> 00:00:37.648
A reader can then fetch a bounded structure keyed by
user.

00:00:37.648 --> 00:00:43.802
This suits a workload with many repeated reads, but
each post now creates work proportional to the

00:00:43.802 --> 00:00:46.564
number of destinations receiving the update.
